# Keep the build context small and reproducible. Both Dockerfiles use the repository
# root as context because they copy Directory.Build.props, the solution file and
# sibling projects, so everything excluded here has to be unnecessary, not just large.

# Local PostgreSQL volume: the single biggest win, and it is test data, not source.
data/

# Build output and intermediates. Copying these in makes restore read stale or
# platform-specific assets and defeats layer caching.
**/bin/
**/obj/

# Editor and OS noise
.vs/
.vscode/
.idea/
*.user
*.suo
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Version control and CI
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
azure-pipelines.yml

# Docs and local tooling not needed to build a runtime image
docs/
*.md
!README.md

# Sources of things that are not built into the image. Restore and publish name the MCP and Web
# projects directly, so the test suite and the retrieval evaluation set are never compiled here;
# they only made the context bigger and every `COPY . .` layer different.
#
# tools/ stays: the DbAdmin image is built from it, and excluding it fails that build at its first
# COPY with "packages.lock.json: not found" — a failure nobody sees until the nightly sweep runs,
# because the image is built by the pack rather than by the gate that checks the other three.
tests/
eval/
.githooks/
.config/
.freebuff/

# Local secrets and logs. A build layer keeps what it was handed even when the file is absent from
# the final stage, so a .env with a real key in the context is a key in the cache on this machine and
# in anything that pushes the layer. None of these belong in an image.
.env
.env.*
*.log
**/TestResults/
